let the embedding app redirect the SIGSYS log

The fork's SIGSYS default case (a blocked syscall with no downgrade
path) appends one line per event to
/data/data/id.or.oo.pr/cache/sigsys-log.txt - the fork's own app's
cache, unwritable for any other embedding app.  For those apps the
fopen fails, the diagnostic silently disappears, and every future
"blocked syscall we did not know about" investigation starts from
zero logcat evidence.  This is exactly why the rename() ENOSYS took
days instead of minutes: the apt failure left no SIGSYS trace at all.

Read the log path from PROOT_SIGSYS_LOG instead: unset keeps the old
default (the fork's own app is unaffected), set to a writable path
redirects the file, set to empty disables file logging.  The app sets
it to a file under its userspace root in ProotRuntime.baseEnv().

Validated on x86_64 Linux with a seccomp filter TRAPping __NR_syslog
(not in the downgrade table): with PROOT_SIGSYS_LOG=<writable path>,
the event line lands in that file; without it, nothing is written;
the ENOSYS result to the tracee is unchanged either way.

Not upstreamed: no write access to oonid/pr; applied by
fetchLinuxSource in linux/build.gradle.kts (stacks on 0001).
--- a/src/proot/src/tracee/seccomp.c
+++ b/src/proot/src/tracee/seccomp.c
